1. Measures the pins.
2. Extracts the relevant clauses from F1/F2a/F2b/C0.
3. Tests the semantic question: is the manifold category / iota regularity *determined* by other pinned content (clause (d) C2 metric + class token exactly C2 + containment ordering), i.e. is the class well-defined without the explicit token?
4. Tests the opposite: is there a concrete pair of extension sets that differ only on manifold category / iota regularity yet both satisfy F2a's literal clauses — i.e. a witness of under-specification? This is the falsifier-driven way. For example: an extension with M' a C2 (non-smooth-structure-fixed) manifold with a C2 metric, and an embedding iota that is C2 but not C3. Under F2b (which requires smooth M' + C∞ isometric embedding), such an extension would NOT count; under F2a's literal text, it WOULD count — yet the class token says "C2". So does the class intend to *include* C2-but-not-smooth extensions (then F2a is right and F2b is the stricter C0 sibling... wait no, F2b is C0 and should be *weaker* in extension regularity, i.e. F2b's extension set E_C0 is LARGER than E_C2).

Hold on, important: the containment ordering. Line 148: "Containment of extension sets runs E_C2 ⊂ E_{C^1,1} ⊂ E_H2loc ⊂ E_C0, which is why lower-regularity inextendibility entails this conclusion". So E_C0 (F2b) is the LARGEST set (weakest inextendibility), E_C2 (F2a) is the smallest. WAIT. That's the opposite of what I said. Let me re-read: "Containment of extension sets runs E_C2 subset of E_{C^1,1} subset of E_H2loc subset of E_C0". So E_C2 ⊂ E_C0: a C2 extension is a special case of a C0 extension. So F2b (C0) forbids more (any C0 extension), F2a (C2) forbids only C2 extensions. Yes: F2a's conclusion "no C2 extension" is implied by F2b's "no C0 extension". So F2b (C0) is the STRONGER conclusion, F2a (C2) weaker. That matches the C0 file line 246 issue: "C2 is a strictly larger extension class, so C2-inextendibility is strictly weaker" — wait, E_C2 ⊂ E_C0 means E_C2 is the *smaller* extension class, so C2-inextendibility (no C2 extension) is *weaker*. Hmm: "C2 is a strictly larger extension class" — if read as "the C2 extension predicate admits a larger class of extensions" that's wrong; E_C2 is smaller. So my HF-W043E-2 stands: should be "strictly smaller".

OK so back to the category question. Since F2b is the C0 class (largest extension set, weakest regularity), it pins M' SMOOTH + C∞ iota (per worker-028's claim at line 190). Hmm, if F2b is the C0 class, why would it require SMOOTH manifold and C∞ embedding? Because "C0" refers to the *metric* regularity g' being C0, and a C0 metric still needs a smooth manifold structure to be defined (a C0 Lorentzian metric on a topological manifold needs at least a C0 atlas; classical Lorentzian geometry typically assumes smooth manifold). And F2b explicitly pins it after an accepted repair (F2b-16-03): "a merely topological M' cannot carry a classical Lorentzian [metric]". So F2b's rationale: even for the C0 extension class, the *manifold* must be smooth (so that a classical Lorentzian metric is definable), while g' is only C0.

For F2a, the extension class is C2: clause (d) g' is C2. A C2 metric also requires a smooth (or at least C2) manifold — and a C2 manifold canonically determines a smooth structure. So the strict reading: F2a's clause (c) "connected 4-manifold" is under-specified relative to F2b's explicit pin, but semantically *equivalent* because:
